{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act criminal record screening limits","requirement":"A housing provider may not inquire about criminal record before a conditional offer, and after the offer may only consider certain convictions within 6, 4, or 1 years, or lifetime sex offender registration. Must conduct individualized assessment before withdrawing an offer.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental dwelling units, except owner-occupied premises with not more than four dwelling units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Excludes owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units. Pre-offer inquiries permitted for methamphetamine production in federally assisted housing and lifetime sex offender registration.","penalty":"Up to $1,000 (no prior violation in 5 years), $5,000 (one prior), $10,000 (two or more priors in 7 years); director may require cease-and-desist, compliance reporting, conditional offer, return of application fee, and up to $1,000 to applicant.","effective_date":"2022-01-01","citation":"C.46:8-55","quoted_span":"A housing provider shall not make any oral or written inquiry regarding an applicant’s criminal record prior to making a conditional offer.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act application fee disclosure","requirement":"Before accepting any application fee, a housing provider must disclose in writing whether criminal history will be considered and the applicant's right to provide evidence of inaccuracies, rehabilitation, or mitigating factors. The director may order return of the fee for violations.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental dwelling units, except owner-occupied premises with not more than four dwelling units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Excludes owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","penalty":"Up to $1,000, $5,000, or $10,000 depending on prior violations; director may require return of the application fee.","effective_date":"2022-01-01","citation":"C.46:8-55","quoted_span":"Prior to accepting any application fee, a housing provider shall disclose in writing to the applicant: (1) Whether the eligibility criteria of the housing provider include the review and consideration of criminal history; and (2) A statement that the applicant, pursuant to subsection a. of this section, may provide evidence demonstrating inaccuracies within the applicant's criminal record or evidence of rehabilitation or other mitigating factors.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act eviction after successful appeal","requirement":"If a housing provider successfully appeals a director's order to provide housing to an applicant, the provider may evict that person if they reside in the unit as a result of the order, with at least 45 days' notice.","key_value":"45 days' notice","coverage_conditions":"Former applicant residing in a rental dwelling unit as a result of a director's order under the Fair Chance in Housing Act.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Applies to former applicants placed under a director's order."},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2022-01-01","citation":"C.46:8-63","quoted_span":"a determination that the housing provider did not violate the provisions of this act as evidenced by such successful appeal shall be grounds for the housing provider to evict the former applicant if that person resides in a rental dwelling unit owned by the housing provider as the result of the director’s order, so long as the housing provider provides the applicant with at least 45 days’ notice prior to the eviction;","interaction":"Notwithstanding the Anti-Eviction Act, P.L.1974, c.49.","confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
